WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.606
Mapping JSON or YAML into Python objects is convenient, but it can become
risky when input fields are treated as trusted program state.

2
00:00:07.606 --> 00:00:15.348
Mass assignment, also called over-posting in many contexts, happens when
input can set fields the application did not intend to expose.

3
00:00:15.348 --> 00:00:20.262
The issue is not the mapping library; it is the lack of an allowlist and a
clear boundary.

4
00:00:20.262 --> 00:00:34.734
User-controlled structured data should not be able to change internal
state, permissions, feature flags, file paths, URLs, command options,
runtime settings, or deployment behavior unless that is the explicit
purpose and the value has been validated.

5
00:00:34.734 --> 00:00:41.062
A safe design separates public input models from internal domain models
and privileged configuration models.

6
00:00:41.062 --> 00:00:47.389
Configuration injection at a high level means structured input influences
configuration in unintended ways.

7
00:00:47.389 --> 00:00:55.669
A field might change a destination URL, enable a feature, select a file
path, alter logging behavior, modify permissions, or change how a job
runs.

8
00:00:55.669 --> 00:00:58.159
These values require more than type checks.

9
00:00:58.159 --> 00:01:04.150
They need allowlisted keys, safe defaults, business validation, and
authorization where appropriate.

10
00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:08.660
Applications should not blindly turn structured input into trusted
behavior.

11
00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:16.065
Prefer narrow constructors, explicit field mapping, immutable defaults
where useful, and reviewable configuration surfaces.

12
00:01:16.065 --> 00:01:25.960
When a new key is added, the team should understand who can set it, what
it controls, how it is validated, how it is logged, and what happens if it
is missing or maliciously shaped.
